as our nation struggles to confront  racial inequality artists are of course  also trying to come to terms with it all  and America's inaugural youth poet  laureate is one of those artists Amanda  Gorman captured this pivotal moment in  an original poem she performed it for  our cameras at the cross colors exhibit  at the California african-american  Museum in Los Angeles  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you will be told this is not the problem  not your problem you will be told that  now is not the time for change to begin  told that we cannot win but the point of  protest isn't winning it's holding fast  to the promise of freedom even when fast  victory isn't promised  meaning we cannot stand up to police if  we cannot cease policing our imagination  convincing our communities that this  won't work before the work has even  begun that this can wait when we've  already waited out a thousand son  by now we understand that white  supremacy and the despair it demands are  as destructive as any disease so when  you're told that your anger is  reactionary remember that rage is our  right it teaches us it is time to fight  in the face of injustice  [Music]  [Applause]  not only is anger natural but necessary  because it helps carry us to a  destination our goal has never been  revenge just restoration not dominance  just dignity not fear just freedom just  justice the greatness of America is the  right to protest  [Music]  whether we prevail is determined not by  all the challenges that are present but  by all the change that is possible and  while we are unstoppable if we ever feel  like we might fail if we be fatigued and  frail when our fire can no longer be  fueled by fury we will be fortified by  this faith found in the anthem the vow  all black lives matter black lives are  worth living worth defending worth every  struggle we owe it to the Fallen to  fight  [Music]  but we owe it to ourselves to never stay  kneeling when the day calls us to stand  together we envision a land that is  liberated and not lawless we create a  future that is free not flawless over  and over again and again we will stride  up every mountainside magnanimous and  modest we will be protected and served  by a forest that is honoured and honest  this is more than protest it's a promise  that's all we can all do when someone  says something that powerful just once  again Amanda Gorman is echo she always  does knocks it out of the park she  always seizes a moment and puts it in a  way that is very unique to her also  shout out shout out shout out to Kris  bender who produced that piece and Brian  Cunningham who edited I say this all the  time those pieces are very hard to do  that way yep Amanda Gorman just never  ceases yes
 

